---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Array PSF designer" description="Design a MIMO radar antenna layout in your browser and see its virtual array, beam pattern, sidelobes, grating lobes and range-angle point-spread function." wide>
  <h1>Array PSF designer</h1>
  <p class="lead">Where you put the antennas decides how sharply a radar can see.</p>
  <p>A MIMO radar with M transmitters and N receivers behaves like M×N virtual antennas, each at the sum of a Tx and an Rx position. Their layout sets the point-spread function (PSF): how a single point target smears across angle. Start from a TI board or the 4-chip cascade we use in the lab, or click to place your own. Everything is computed in your browser by direct summation, so any geometry works, not only regular grids.</p>

  <div class="tool">
    <div class="panel controls" data-frame>
      <fieldset>
        <legend>Array</legend>
        <label>Start from <select id="preset"></select></label>
        <label>Units
          <select id="unit">
            <option value="half">half-wavelengths (λ/2)</option>
            <option value="lambda">wavelengths (λ)</option>
            <option value="mm">millimetres</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label for="tx">Tx positions, one "x y" per line</label>
        <textarea id="tx" rows="5" spellcheck="false"></textarea>
        <label for="rx">Rx positions</label>
        <textarea id="rx" rows="5" spellcheck="false"></textarea>
        <label><input type="checkbox" id="merge" checked> Count overlapping virtual elements once</label>
        <p class="note" id="errs" role="alert"></p>
      </fieldset>
      <fieldset>
        <legend>Signal</legend>
        <div class="row">
          <label>Carrier <input type="number" id="f0" min="1" max="300" step="0.25" value="77" style="width:5.5em"> GHz</label>
          <label>Bandwidth <input type="number" id="bw" min="0.01" max="20" step="0.1" value="4" style="width:5em"> GHz</label>
        </div>
        <label>Window (angle and range)
          <select id="win">
            <option value="none">none (uniform)</option>
            <option value="hann">Hann</option>
            <option value="hamming">Hamming</option>
            <option value="blackman">Blackman</option>
          </select>
        </label>
      </fieldset>
      <fieldset>
        <legend>Steering and display</legend>
        <label>Steer azimuth <span class="val" data-val="az0">0°</span><input type="range" id="az0" min="-70" max="70" step="1" value="0"></label>
        <label>Steer elevation <span class="val" data-val="el0">0°</span><input type="range" id="el0" min="-70" max="70" step="1" value="0"></label>
        <label>Dynamic range <span class="val" data-val="dyn">40 dB</span><input type="range" id="dyn" min="10" max="80" step="5" value="40"></label>
      </fieldset>
    </div>

    <div class="stage">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Layout</h3>
      <div class="row">
        <label>Click on the layout to
          <select id="click">
            <option value="tx">add a Tx</option>
            <option value="rx">add an Rx</option>
            <option value="remove">remove the nearest</option>
            <option value="none">do nothing</option>
          </select>
        </label>
      </div>
      <svg id="layout" class="plot" role="img" aria-label="Antenna layout: filled triangles are transmitters, open circles are receivers, grey dots are virtual elements" style="width:100%;cursor:crosshair;margin-top:8px"></svg>
      <p class="note">▲ Tx &nbsp; ○ Rx &nbsp; <span style="opacity:.7">●</span> virtual elements. Grid: one unit.</p>

      <div class="stats" id="stats" aria-live="polite"></div>
      <p class="note" id="msg" aria-live="polite"></p>

      <div class="photos2" style="align-items:start">
        <div>
          <h3>Beam pattern</h3>
          <canvas id="uv" class="pixelated" style="width:100%;aspect-ratio:1" role="img" aria-label="Two-dimensional beam pattern over the visible hemisphere, darker is stronger"></canvas>
          <p class="note">The whole visible half-space seen from the front, in direction cosines: left–right is azimuth, up–down elevation, the rim is 90°. Darker means stronger.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Range × azimuth</h3>
          <canvas id="ra" style="width:100%;aspect-ratio:1" role="img" aria-label="Range-azimuth point-spread function, darker is stronger"></canvas>
          <p class="note" id="ralabel"></p>
          <p class="note">Far-field approximation: range and angle separate, so this is the range profile times the azimuth cut.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Cuts through the beam</h3>
      <svg id="cuts" class="plot" role="img" aria-label="Azimuth and elevation cuts of the beam pattern in decibels" style="width:100%"></svg>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 data-under>What to look for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Beamwidth</strong> shrinks as the aperture grows: about 0.89 λ/D radians for a uniform line of length D. The cascade's 86-element line gives roughly 1.2°.</li>
    <li><strong>Sidelobes</strong>: a uniform line sits near −13 dB; a Hann or Hamming taper pushes them down and widens the main lobe.</li>
    <li><strong>Grating lobes</strong> appear when elements are more than λ/2 apart: try the "λ spacing" preset, and steer it.</li>
    <li><strong>Elevation</strong> comes only from vertical offsets. The xWR1843's raised Tx2 adds four elevated virtual elements; the cascade has a few more.</li>
    <li>The virtual array assumes a far-field target and that the Tx and Rx phase centres are where you put them. Real boards also need phase calibration.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">I wrote the first version in Python to read the PSF of a real 12×16 array from a photo of its antenna layout. A radiation-pattern viewer for any array had been on my ideas list for a while.</p>
</Base>
<script>
  import '../../scripts/tools/psf';
</script>
